// @title   TropeLang Concept Library — Emotional
// @version 1.3
// @note    Emotions as concept nodes — distinct from emotional states ([#Mind]).
//          A character IN a state of grief uses [#Grieving].
//          A character who FEARS grief, SEEKS joy, or is DEVOTED to love
//          uses a concept node as the target of a [~Intent] relationship.
//          These concepts also appear in edges: grief -- loss : "Caused By".
//
// Import: import "trl/concepts/emotional.trl"


// =============================================================================
// PRIMARY EMOTIONS AS CONCEPTS
// These name the emotion as a thing in the world, not just an internal state.
// =============================================================================

// The experience of intense positive feeling; a heightened sense of aliveness and well-being.
// Joy as concept: something a character may seek, fear losing, or be devoted to.
concept joy            [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The experience of intense negative feeling arising from significant loss or harm.
// Grief as concept: something a character may avoid, fear, or carry as a burden.
concept grief          [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// The sense of threat or anticipated harm; the response to perceived danger.
// Fear as concept: something a character may seek to master, flee from, or weaponize.
concept fear           [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// The desire or expectation of a positive outcome; forward-directed positive feeling.
// Hope as concept: something that can be lost, given, destroyed, or sustained.
concept hope           [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract] [+Fragile]

// The condition in which no positive outcome appears possible; the absence of hope.
// Despair as concept: something a character may fall into, resist, or transmit to others.
concept despair        [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract] [+Corrupting]

// A wounding that persists past its cause — the lingering psychic injury of overwhelming harm.
// Trauma as concept: something a character carries, relives, or is undone by (the discovery node the
// trauma-arc tropes — Heroic BSOD, Survivor's Guilt — reach for; cf. the `Trauma` attr).
concept trauma         [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract] [+Corrupting]

// The directed desire toward another being or thing; an orientation of wanting and care.
// Love as concept: distinct from love as a state — here it is something pursued, lost, or built.
concept love           [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Generative] [+Fragile]

// The sustained negative orientation toward another; a desire for their harm or absence.
// Hate as concept: something a character may nurture, overcome, or be consumed by.
concept hate           [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// The desire for what another has; resentment of their advantage or possession.
// Envy as concept: something a character acts from, denies, or is destroyed by.
concept envy           [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// The desire or expectation of a negative outcome; backward-looking negative feeling about future.
// Dread as concept: something a character lives under, fights, or is paralyzed by.
concept dread          [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// COMPLEX EMOTIONAL STATES AS CONCEPTS
// =============================================================================

// The persistent ache for something absent — a person, place, time, or state.
// Longing differs from desire in its orientation toward what cannot currently be had.
concept longing        [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// A form of longing directed toward the past — desire to return to a prior time or place.
// Nostalgia idealizes what is remembered; the past it reaches for may not have existed as recalled.
concept nostalgia      [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract] [+Paradoxical]

// The inner conflict arising when one's actions contradict one's values.
// Shame as concept: something a character conceals, is driven by, or learns to release.
concept shame          [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// The inner conflict arising from having caused harm to another.
// Guilt differs from shame: shame is about who one is; guilt is about what one has done.
concept guilt          [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// The quality of treating others as they wish to be treated; imaginative self-placement in another's experience.
// Empathy as concept: something a character may practice, lose, or be denied.
concept empathy        [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The condition of holding contradictory feelings or orientations simultaneously.
// Ambivalence as concept: something a character lives with, resolves, or is paralyzed by.
concept ambivalence    [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Paradoxical]

// The heightened state of positive feeling when one reflects on one's own actions or attributes.
// Pride as concept (healthy): something a character earns, maintains, or loses. See virtues.trl for pride_excess.
concept pride          [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The experience of being known and accepted despite one's flaws or past.
// Being forgiven is passive — the concept names what is received, not given.
concept being_forgiven [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The state of having one's experience witnessed and validated by another.
// Being seen requires both a witness and an accurate perception; it cannot be self-conferred.
concept being_seen     [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// EMOTIONAL CONDITIONS THAT CHARACTERS DIRECT THEMSELVES TOWARD
// Common targets of [~Intent] relationships
// =============================================================================

// The condition of being at peace with present circumstances; wanting no other state.
// Contentment is often invisible — it leaves no trace in narrative until it is threatened.
concept contentment    [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract] [+Fragile]

// The experience of being cared for, nurtured, or kept safe by another.
// To be comforted is to have suffering witnessed and met with care.
concept comfort        [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The state of existing within a bounded, protected space — free from threat.
// Security is both material (safety from harm) and existential (certainty of place).
concept security       [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract] [+Fragile]

// The condition of being without constraint or obligation — able to move, choose, and act.
// Freedom-as-feeling differs from freedom-as-condition; the first can exist without the second.
concept liberation     [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The experience of pleasure, delight, or amusement; levity in the face of existence.
// Joy is intense and exceptional; happiness is quieter and sustained.
concept happiness      [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]
